  <section class="section container prose">
    <span class="tag">Knowledge · Sourcing</span>
    <h1>China battery industry regions</h1>
    <p class="byline">Published by Dingwei Battery · Last reviewed 2026-09-10 · Source: industry cluster research; verify individual factories independently</p>
    <p class="lead">China’s lead-acid battery industry is not one market — it is five or six regional clusters, each with its own product mix, cost structure and export posture. Shortlisting by region gets you a tighter, more comparable set of candidates than shortlisting by search ranking.</p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> China’s lead-acid battery production clusters in roughly 5 regions — Hebei (Jinzhou/Shijiazhuang, where Dingwei’s parent Chengguang Power Tech is based), Shandong (Linyi/Weifang), Jiangsu, Zhejiang (Changxing, the traditional domestic heartland) and Anhui (Jieshou, a recycled-lead centre). Zhejiang’s Changxing hosts 2 of China’s largest domestic producers, but a cluster is not a quality grade — every cluster contains both export-grade plants and low-end rebuilders, so verify each factory individually.
    </div>

    <h2>Why the industry clusters</h2>
    <p>Lead-acid production clusters for three structural reasons that have nothing to do with marketing. First, the supply chain is heavy and local: recycled lead smelting, sulphuric acid, separators and grid alloy all want to sit close to the plant to keep freight down. Second, the workforce is specialised — a region builds a pool of plate-casting and formation operators that a new entrant can hire on day one. Third, environmental permitting is easier inside a designated industrial park, which is where the clusters live.</p>
    <p>For a buyer, the practical consequence is that two factories an hour apart share suppliers and labour but can sit a full price tier apart. The region narrows the field; it does not grade the players.</p>

    <h2>The major clusters</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Region</th><th>Key areas</th><th>What it is known for</th><th>Buyer note</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Hebei</td><td>Jinzhou, Shijiazhuang belt</td><td>Export-focused OEM and private-label production; deep automotive battery experience</td><td>{MANUFACTURER.shortName} is based here — an example of the region’s export posture.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shandong</td><td>Linyi, Weifang</td><td>Large-scale lead recycling and mid-volume production</td><td>Strong on recycled-lead supply; verify the grade of lead, not just the volume.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Jiangsu</td><td>Various industrial parks</td><td>Higher-cost, process-driven plants; export and OEM friendly</td><td>Often strong on documentation and certification discipline.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Zhejiang</td><td>Changxing (Huzhou)</td><td>The traditional domestic heartland; large branded producers</td><td>Home to major domestic brands; the biggest names may be less interested in small OEM runs.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Anhui</td><td>Jieshou (Tianying)</td><td>Recycled-lead smelting and battery reconditioning</td><td>Strong on cost and material recovery; inspect process control carefully.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>How to use the region in shortlisting</h2>
    <p>The region is a filter, not a verdict. Use it in three ways:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Match the product to the region</strong> — if you need a big-brand domestic supplier, Zhejiang; if you need an export OEM partner willing to run private label, the Hebei/Jiangsu posture often fits better; if cost on recycled lead matters most, look at Shandong or Anhui.</li>
      <li><strong>Compare within a region first</strong> — two factories sharing a lead supplier and a labour pool quote more comparably than factories 1,000 km apart. An apples-to-apples RFQ comparison is easier inside one cluster.</li>
      <li><strong>Never let the region do the verifying</strong> — the same cluster contains export-grade plants and rebuilders. Run the <a href="/knowledge/battery-supplier-verification/">supplier verification</a> on every candidate regardless of postcode.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What the region does not tell you</h2>
    <p>A cluster raises the floor on infrastructure — the roads, the recycled-lead supply, the permit environment — but it raises nothing about your specific supplier. The mistakes buyers make are the reverse of what they expect:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Assuming a famous cluster means quality</strong> — the weak plants inside a strong cluster still exist, and they price accordingly.</li>
      <li><strong>Assuming a less famous cluster means weak quality</strong> — some of the most disciplined export plants sit outside the headline regions.</li>
      <li><strong>Paying a cluster premium without knowing it</strong> — brand-name regions can carry a price premium that does not show up in the specification.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The only reliable way to separate the two is the same three-layer check everywhere: the business licence, the certificates and the capacity — covered in the <a href="/knowledge/battery-supplier-verification/">supplier verification</a> guide.</p>

    <h2>Region, then specification, then price</h2>
    <p>The sourcing order matters. Choose the region to build a credible shortlist, lock the specification in the <a href="/knowledge/battery-specifications-guide/">specifications reference</a>, then score candidates with the <a href="/knowledge/how-choose-oem-battery-manufacturer/">how to choose a manufacturer</a> framework. Only after that does price become meaningful — comparing prices across regions without a fixed specification is comparing different batteries.</p>
    <p>{RELATIONSHIP} The full sequence lives in the <a href="/knowledge/battery-sourcing-guide/">sourcing guide</a>; see the <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-rfq-guide/">RFQ guide</a> for how to keep a multi-region comparison apples-to-apples.</p>
  </section>


    <h2>The clusters, one by one</h2>
    <p>The table above is the overview; the short profiles below are what a buyer actually needs to shortlist. Treat these as directional &mdash; the individual factory always matters more than its postcode.</p>
    <h3>Hebei &mdash; Jinzhou / Shijiazhuang belt</h3>
    <p>The Jinzhou area sits in the Shijiazhuang orbit and has long been an export-oriented battery manufacturing base. Plants here tend to be structured around OEM and private-label programs for overseas buyers rather than chasing the domestic replacement market &mdash; which means export documentation, multilingual labels and container logistics are routine rather than special requests. {MANUFACTURER.shortName} is here: a {MANUFACTURER.factoryArea} plant with {MANUFACTURER.productionLines} producing {MANUFACTURER.dailyOutput}, exporting from {MANUFACTURER.exportPorts}.</p>
    <h3>Shandong &mdash; Linyi / Weifang</h3>
    <p>Shandong is a recycled-lead powerhouse. Large smelting capacity sits alongside battery production, which means material cost can be competitive &mdash; but it also means the grade of lead matters more, and a buyer should confirm the alloy and paste specification rather than assume recycled lead means lower quality. The region leans toward volume production at aggressive price points.</p>
    <h3>Jiangsu</h3>
    <p>Jiangsu plants tend to sit a price tier higher and compete on process discipline and documentation rather than raw cost. If your market demands certification-heavy compliance (automotive OEM, strict testing regimes), a Jiangsu plant is often easier to work with on the paperwork side. The trade-off is a higher unit cost for that discipline.</p>
    <h3>Zhejiang &mdash; Changxing (Huzhou)</h3>
    <p>Changxing is the traditional domestic heartland and home to some of China&rsquo;s largest branded producers. The consequence for a buyer is double-edged: the process knowledge is deep, but the biggest names are often oriented to their own brands and domestic volume, and may be less interested in a smaller OEM or private-label run. The mid-sized plants in the same cluster often combine the process strength with the appetite for export work.</p>
    <h3>Anhui &mdash; Jieshou (Tianying)</h3>
    <p>Jieshou is a recycled-lead and reconditioning centre. Cost is the headline advantage, but it is the cluster where process control varies most between plants &mdash; so an audit is not optional here. Treat an Anhui candidate as viable only after the <a href="/knowledge/how-audit-battery-factory/">factory audit</a> confirms curing, formation and testing discipline.</p>

    <h2>Port access and the logistics angle</h2>
    <p>The cluster&rsquo;s distance to a major port shapes your freight cost and lead time. Hebei ships naturally through {MANUFACTURER.exportPorts.split(' / ')[0]}; Shandong and Jiangsu are close to Qingdao and Shanghai; Zhejiang is near Ningbo and Shanghai; Anhui routes inland to Shanghai. A factory two hours from a port turns a container faster and cheaper than one that trucks goods 800&nbsp;km inland &mdash; a real cost that does not appear in the unit price but shows up in the landed cost.</p>

    <h2>How price tiers map to regions</h2>
    <p>Region is a rough proxy for price tier, not a guarantee. As a working guide: Anhui and Shandong often price at the aggressive end (cost-driven, recycled-lead heavy), Jiangsu at the higher end (process-driven), with Hebei and the mid-sized Zhejiang plants in between and often the strongest fit for export OEM. The only reliable way to turn that into a decision is a fixed specification and a <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-rfq-guide/">comparable RFQ</a> &mdash; a price comparison across regions without a locked specification is comparing different batteries.</p>



    <h2>How to verify a regional claim</h2>
    <p>Because a cluster is not a quality grade, the region on the brochure has to be checked the same way as everything else on it. Three quick verifications tie the &ldquo;where&rdquo; to the &ldquo;who&rdquo;:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>The registered address matches the plant you are told about</strong> &mdash; a supplier claiming a Zhejiang plant but registered to an office in a different city is not what it appears.</li>
      <li><strong>The business scope names production</strong> &mdash; a trading company can describe any region; only a producer can legally run a line there.</li>
      <li><strong>The port logic holds</strong> &mdash; a Hebei plant shipping through Tianjin is normal; a &ldquo;Hebei&rdquo; supplier routing everything through Shenzhen deserves a question.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>These are the same three checks in the <a href="/knowledge/battery-supplier-verification/">supplier verification</a> guide, applied through the lens of geography. A region is only useful if the factory is actually in it.</p>

    <h2>Sources and further reading</h2>
    <ul>
